text=f'''
## Round 2

Screened 30 October 2026. **The CSV now has {len(rows)} distinct papers: {len(eligible)} eligible and {len(rows)-len(eligible)} excluded.** Added {len(new)} rows (6 eligible, 15 excluded), reassessed two existing leads in depth, and promoted the existing I4R-9c8p6 row on new historical-version evidence. Thus round 2 contributes **7 newly eligible papers**: five computational issues, one technical failure, and one ambiguous. No new success candidates were admitted. `round` records the first logged screening round; the promoted I4R-9c8p6 remains `round=1`, with its round-2 decision in notes. All 90 original rows remain.

**Updated eligible pool by distribution source**

{table(eligible)}

**Round-2 eligibility gains** (including the promoted round-1 record)

{table(gains)}

Targets remain unmet: computational issues **12/15**, technical failure **3/8**, ambiguous **8/9**. I stopped these searches after the effort below rather than weakening the rules. A workable proposed 30-paper composition is **12 reproducible / 10 computational issues / 3 technical failures / 5 ambiguous**. This leaves two issue replacements and three ambiguous replacements, but no technical-failure reserve; pin and adjudicate all three P0 cases before any draw. Keeping five real technical failures requires further qualifying papers, not relabelling secondary faults or adding seeded twins inside the 30.

The 40% source cap is dropped, as instructed. Underlying human evidence remains **47/49 I4R**, with one SCORE and one CODECHECK case; the REPRO-Bench rows predominantly redistribute I4R evidence. Report this dependence, the predominance of R, and the availability-based sampling as limitations.

**Where I looked and what “screened” counts.** There are 21 new complete CSV screening records plus two substantive reassessments (I4R-9c8p6 and SC-GJe4), i.e. **23 logged round-2 case assessments**. Broad discovery work is separate from this count; reading a catalogue entry or automated keyword hit is not claimed as a full paper screen.

- **I4R first:** reused the 416-record catalogue and 153 previously indexed report records, excluding already screened papers from the targeted search. Queried 243 additional OSF catalogue records; 139 yielded report text, representing 138 distinct nodes. Text triage searched original-code reproduction sections for numerical differences, missing inputs, errors, RNG seeds, convergence and version dependence. Close-read the leads retained in the new CSV and the source sections referenced here; successful reproductions were not added. Also opened [DP287](https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/240013/1/I4R-DP287.pdf) to recover human summaries and actual report links where catalogue nodes contained only code or were empty. Raw listings, retrieval errors and local reports are retained in `round2_remaining_index.json`, `round2_fetch.log`, `round2_reports/` and `round2_unscreened_sections.txt`. Seven additional catalogue records had retrieval errors; failed requests are not evidence of a missing scientific input.
- **REPRO-Bench next:** re-filtered every score-1/2 HF task not already logged, using actual main-analysis filenames rather than accepting any `.py` experiment code as Python analysis. Seventeen new inventory leads had an R/Python file; most also required Stata/MATLAB, and task 108 exceeded 5 GB. The R-only leads were 1, 10, 94, 98, 101, 102, 111 and 112. Human evidence for 1/10 reproduces original results; 94’s main differences follow corrected coding/specifications; 98’s available material did not establish a qualified unchanged-code discrepancy; 101’s erratum corrects inputs and model specification and has licensed Ethnologue-source limitations; 102’s retraction concerns experimental randomization rather than a verified computational nonrecurrence; 112’s report recovers numbers despite a table-generation problem. These do not create eligible new issue cases. Task 111 was the gain: completed the previously truncated inventory across six pages, **5,855 entries / 607,938,492 bytes**, with only two R scripts. Its human report is [DP151 revised](https://www.econstor.eu/bitstream/10419/306278/1/I4R-DP151rev.pdf). The complete inventory and page links are in `round2_packages/hf111_full_inventory.json`; no 608-MB bundle download was needed.
- **SCORE:** re-filtered the analyst archive’s version-of-record rows. It contains **104 `not` and five `not attemptable` claims**, rather than the brief’s rounded/earlier count of 100. These occur in 53 papers. Eleven papers combine a negative outcome, reported public original code and Push Button/Extended Push Button reproduction: 8R9d, Pxp7, LyWB, 9DZl, a2Yx, L22B, 9XrX, WLkV, QYNq, GJe4 and R0ak. Re-read the claim descriptions; inspected the seven remaining original/reproducer inventories and human reports as available. Most are Stata/non-R workflows. No new SCORE candidate qualified. For GJe4 the actual report and console log now identify a missing saved-RNG-state file, but an included original script creates it (see below). For 9XrX, the [later report](https://osf.io/download/e629y/) explicitly identifies Model 3 and successfully reproduces the >.98 posterior probability. Do not import the earlier export’s `not attemptable` label. `round2_score_negative_pool.json`, `round2_score_inventory.log` and `round2_packages/score_remaining_report_audit.json` preserve this audit.
- **Other public source:** opened the [CODECHECK register](https://codecheck.org.uk/register/) and ten relevant certificates: 2021-003, 2023-001, 2023-004, 2023-008, 2024-018, 2024-019, 2024-020, 2025-005, 2026-023 and 2026-026. Read detailed checker notes for the five apparent issue/blocker leads, inventoried associated repositories, and retrieved original versioned data/code for 2023-004. Only that case was added as eligible. Several others were clean successes, export-only limitations, later supplied notebooks, or downsampled simulations with no full-runtime evidence. Their success certificates were not used to add more reproducible papers.

**Settled decisions applied.** RB-065 and RB-038 have medium confidence and the requested warning that the reference reproduction used another software/library and does not establish execution of the shipped R code. SC-8R9d has `acceptable_alternative_status=technical failure`. I4R-eb89k is in-scope behavioural genetics; primary Aims 2/3 are blocked, so retain technical failure. Its current pinned commit and sampled original R/CSV files still contain the source-described variable-name mismatch; hashes are in `round2_packages/eb89k_round2_static_audit.json`. I4R-mepbv retains its pinned, statically verified manuscript parse blocker. Added `code_format=embedded` to I4R-bz8pf and I4R-mgy45 and recorded the final cap of two. RB-019 stays reproducible with acceptable alternative computational issues (P2/P3 on the figure). The two reply-disputed exclusions, I4R-8dxvk and I4R-xqnhw, remain excluded.

**New judgement calls for integration (five).**

1. **CC-2023-004: genuine primary P0, independently sourced.** The [human report](https://osf.io/download/3c2rx/) records missing GMHWG code/columns and subsequent correspondence fixes. Both Figshare versions are retrievable; neither inspected script creates `GMHWG`, yet primary between-service comparisons and correlations require it. Evaluating the unchanged expression with the observed Group 1 data confirms `object 'GMHWG' not found`. Gold refers to **Figshare 22132940 v1**, code file **39347480**, SHA-256 `ce9f3a018da08164caa62e0b601399ee740bed9b2f2cd4311bfb47bbf3c4fa5e`; data archive **39347477**, SHA-256 `86898d52d894af27904f3e80428c71a32af4f8a42f3bc575ad30138caf8ee89e`. Earlier claims may match; affected primary claims are `not_checkable` + P0. Map review/final exhibits by content because numbering differs. This is a static blocker check, not a complete numerical rerun.
2. **I4R-9c8p6: recoverable historical secondary P0, hence issues.** Recovered **OSF script revision 6**, SHA-256 `ecedfe3161827b762c787a4e6b066e67d64eda8c348ff2672d6a00f307214f0d`. Revision 7 changes only the called activity-data filename. The broken block describes activities; primary well-being analyses use another supplied dataset and can run independently. Following your primary-claim rule, the historical package is computational issues, with blocked descriptive claims `not_checkable` + P0. The current fixed package is reproducible. If filename normalization is permitted, reproducible is an acceptable alternative. This promotion rests on the new version evidence; it does not label the fixed deposit a failure.
3. **RB-111: ambiguity is confined to upstream gender inference.** Original R cleaning of archived pages yields eight different inferred-gender records and N=11,018 versus 11,016 across the R versions named by the human source. Use those upstream-dependent claims for abstention; supplied processed-data regressions can be reproducible. Record the alternative scope/status rather than treating the source’s Stata corrections as failures of original R code.
4. **I4R-axj5y and I4R-cyb24: scope the actual nonrecurrences.** For axj5y, the source runs the original R analysis with supplied contextual probabilities; rebuilding probabilities took >5 h and is out of scope. Original R code sets a seed but selects an input RDS by filesystem modification time, so pin input choice before assigning precise discrepancies; ambiguity is an acceptable alternative until that is resolved. For cyb24, use the descriptive percentages identified in its unblind reproduction, alongside matching primary regressions, from public processed CSVs. LIWC feature regeneration requires an unavailable dictionary and is outside the eligible run. The authors’ [DP200 reply](https://www.econstor.eu/bitstream/10419/311305/1/I4R-DP200.pdf) defends the main regressions, without specifically rebutting those percentages. Do not use blind-recoding or semantic-LLM changes as issue evidence.
5. **I4R-u6gea: count discrepancies, not a scientific recoding correction.** The report identifies 131 versus 132 binary outputs and 378 versus 370 subgroup/effect-size tallies. Independently counted **370 original CSV rows, 132 Binary outputs**. Use these reporting discrepancies; do not use the source’s changed effect-size denominator as nonrecurrence. The missing secondary moderator-input file is P0 for that section only. Primary pooled models use the supplied CSV, so the paper remains computational issues. The public human report’s view-only link allowed original code/data downloads; the root analysis files total 0.286 MB, excluding preregistration material.

**Further exclusions worth retaining.** I4R-xfc2m has clear numerical discrepancies but its data were obtained under an NDA. I4R-rvynd’s public R subsection is compelling but the full workflow includes Stata and confidential regressors; no mixed/restricted-workflow exception was made. I4R-a8kp5 retains an old faulty script, but its named typo is in diagnostics after the primary estimates. I4R-nh568’s report reconstructs Study 4 and inconsistently reports its own interaction coefficient; the current package actually contains combining/recoding code, so no reliable P0/reference label was assigned. I4R-vkgtf’s current README links the missing dataframe externally, which was not verified as unavailable. SC-GJe4’s [human report](https://osf.io/download/63bzr/) and [console output](https://osf.io/download/9r72q/) now establish the missing saved seed in `00_replicate.R`; current `10_mainmods.R` can create those seeds, so this does not yet establish a paper-wide P0. CODECHECK 2024-018 reduced 1,000 simulation samples to ten; its 5–10-minute reduced runtime is not evidence for full eligibility. Export failures in CODECHECK 2026-026 do not establish failed underlying numbers.

**Audit and verification.** New rows, source-text paths and report hashes are in `round2_candidate_rows.json` and `round2_candidate_audit.json`; original/versioned packages, source documents, API inventories and checks are under `round2_packages/`. The round-1 CSV was backed up as `round1_candidates.csv`. Verified new eligible DOIs against publisher/institutional records or the original article; Crossref title queries became rate-limited, and those errors were retained rather than treated as matches. The report for I4R-nh568 itself cites a wrong DOI; the excluded row uses the original article’s verified DOI instead. The quantitative comparisons are human-source results, except explicitly labelled static/count checks. Runtime and precise 5–8-claim mapping remain screening estimates, as in round 1. No statistical analyses were replaced to force agreement, no full reproduction success is claimed, no commits were made, and requested outputs are confined to the two permitted directories.
'''
